The Little Hours is a 2017 black comedy film written and directed by Jeff Baena and loosely based on stories
from the third day of The Decameron, a 14th-century collection of novellas by Giovanni Boccaccio.

12
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:06,000
It stars an ensemble cast featuring Alison Brie, Dave
Franco, Aubrey Plaza, Kate Micucci, John C.

13
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
Reilly, Molly Shannon, and Fred Armisen.

14
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:06,000
Set in the 14th century, The Little Hours uses modern dialogue – in the spirit of its
source material – improvised by the actors based on Baena's detailed outline.

15
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:36,000
It focuses on the sex lives of its characters, including nuns at a convent (Brie, Micucci, Plaza and Shannon) in rural Tuscany, a local coven of witches, and
a young servant (Franco) who ends up pretending to be a deaf-mute gardener at the convent after running away from his master (Nick Offerman).

16
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
It premiered at the Sundance Film Festival on January 19, 2017, and was theatrically released
by Gunpowder & Sky, the company's first such distribution, on June 30, 2017.

17
01:16:30,000 --> 01:16:36,000
Muted complaints from Catholic groups did not negatively affect the film's release; it received
generally positive reviews from critics, featuring praise for the cast's performances.

